-- A pinned derivation across modules: `H.eq` is specialised to `Holder Int`,
-- so `M.W a`, which holds a `Holder a`, derives `==` with its parameter
-- PINNED to `Int` (checker-v2.md §11.2), and `M`
-- publishes the pin in its row's scheme. `Main` compares `M.W Int` values
-- directly, through `M.X` (a pin inherited in `M`), through its own `Y`
-- (a pin inherited across the module boundary, by an unannotated `same`
-- whose argument the pin decides) and inside a list. Every comparison goes
-- through `H.eq`, which compares mod 10: 3 and 13 are equal, 3 and 14 are
-- not. None of them is NOT EQUATABLE. Oracle twin: the same
-- comparisons with `W` replaced by a tuple `( H.Holder Int, Int )`.
pub type Holder a
    = Holder a


pub eq : Holder Int, Holder Int → Bool
eq (Holder x) (Holder y) = Int.mod x 10 == Int.mod y 10
